@doc """
A Monad that retains an Error without producing a successful payload.
A failed value does not call bind's mapper.

## Failure in ordinary functions
SafeBind (`=?`) in an ordinary function or closure requires its own return
type to implement MonadFail. An Err on the right-hand side, a Pattern mismatch,
or an Extractor failure supplies an Error to `MonadFail::fail`. The resulting
value is returned immediately; the remaining statements are skipped.

Here the literal Pattern `True` can fail. A mismatch produces a
`BooleanLiteralPatternMismatch` Error, and `MonadFail::fail` for Result constructs `Err(error)`
as the function's return value. The successful path returns `Ok(1)`.

xldr(1)> def require_true(value: Boolean) -> Result<Int> { True =? value; Ok(1) }
xldr(2)> require_true(True)
Ok(1)
xldr(3)> match require_true(False) { Err(error) => Error::kind(error), Ok(_) => "success" }
"BooleanLiteralPatternMismatch"

## Failure in do
SafeBind and partial Patterns in `<-` use the do block's carrier as their
failure target. When it implements MonadFail, the failure Error is passed to
`MonadFail::fail` to construct the do block's result. Otherwise Alternative
supplies `empty()` and discards the Error. If neither capability is available,
these potentially failing statements are rejected. Total `<-` Patterns only
require Monad.

In this Result do block, `<-` matches the payload of `Ok(value)` against `True`.
When it fails, the block returns `Err(error)` without reaching `Ok(1)`.

xldr(1)> def require_true_do(value: Boolean) -> Result<Int> { do { True <- Ok(value); Ok(1) } }
xldr(2)> require_true_do(True)
Ok(1)
xldr(3)> match require_true_do(False) { Err(error) => Error::kind(error), Ok(_) => "success" }
"BooleanLiteralPatternMismatch"

SafeBind automatically unwraps only the outer layer of a canonical Result on
its right-hand side. Implementing MonadFail does not make other carriers
implicitly unwrap there.
"""
deftrait MonadFail
where
  Self: Monad
{
  @doc """
  Construct a failed value that retains the supplied Error and has no
  successful payload. `Self` is the complete result type, selected from the
  expected type or an explicit ReturnTypeArgument. For Result this constructs
  `Err(error)`; for `Either<Error, A>` it constructs `Either::Left(error)`.

  Ordinary functions and do blocks use this operation when a potentially
  failing Pattern or SafeBind produces an Error and the failure target
  implements MonadFail. The original Error is retained.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> value: Result<Int> = MonadFail::fail(NoneError())
  xldr(2)> match value { Err(error) => Error::kind(error), Ok(_) => "success" }
  "NoneError"
  xldr(3)> Result::is_err(MonadFail::fail::<Result<Int>>(NoneError))
  True
  """
  def fail::<Self>(error: Error) -> Self

  @doc """
  Fail when cond is True; otherwise evaluate then. Only the selected lazy
  argument runs, exactly once. Parentheses retain the ordinary eager boundary.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> MonadFail::fail_if(False, NoneError(), Ok(3))
  Ok(3)
  """
  def fail_if(condition: Boolean, err: Lazy<Error>, then: Lazy<Self>) -> Self {
    if(condition, MonadFail::fail(err()), then())
  }
}
